      "title": "ChArUco boards: render, detect, calibrate",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "cv2.aruco.GridBoard, CharucoBoard and CharucoDetector are cv2 classes, so each comes as a curated node rather than a raw cv2.* wrapper.\n\nA ChArUco board interleaves ArUco markers into a chessboard, which is the best of both worlds: the markers say WHICH corner is which (so a partially visible, occluded or edge-clipped board still contributes) while the chessboard corners themselves localise to sub-pixel accuracy (better than marker corners ever do).\n\nRow 1 - render a printable board. The image size is derived from the board's own geometry, so the aspect ratio is always exact - passing a mismatched size is what makes cv2's generateImage throw. The physical marker/square lengths come out as numbers so every pose downstream is in your unit.\nRow 2 - detect it. 'object_points' pairs each identified corner with its 3-D position on the board, which is exactly what cv2.solvePnP wants: wire object_points + corners + a camera matrix into 'CV Solve PnP Pose' for the board pose.\nRow 3 - the GridBoard variant (markers only), whose corners feed the existing 'CV ArUco Board Pose'.\n\nTo calibrate for real: print the board, photograph it from ~15 angles, load them as a batch and feed 'CV Calibrate Camera (ChArUco)'. Because partial views still count, you get far better coverage of the image CORNERS - which is exactly where distortion is estimated - than a plain chessboard allows.\n\nIMPORTANT: cell_size must be the size of the PRINTED board, and legacy_pattern must be on for boards generated by OpenCV before 4.6 (white top-left cell) or the corner ids shift."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
